==German==

===Etymology===
Of {{unk|de|uncertain}} origin. Attested since the 15th century. Several etymologies have been proposed.<ref>[http://etymologiebank.ivdnt.org/trefwoord/schans1 schans], etymologiebank.ivdnt.org</ref>

{{w|Wolfgang Pfeifer (etymologist)|Pfeifer}} suggests the regional {{w|Franconian (linguistics)|Franconian}}/{{w|Hessian dialects|Hessian}} term {{m|gmw-rfr|Schanze|t=basket}} (itself of unknown origin) as the origin, arguing that entrenchments resemble baskets from the inside, being dug-out earth pits that have been reinforced from the inside with brushwood. He rejects a derivation from {{m+|it|scansi|w=1|pos=n}}, plural of {{m|it|scanso}}, claiming that such theories are based on an unverifiable meaning of “defense”/“resistance” of the Italian term.<ref>{{R:de:EWbD}}</ref>

See also {{cog|nl|schans}}, {{cog|da|skanse}}, {{cog|sv|skans}} and {{cog|en|sconce}}.

